---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageTitle from "../components/PageTitle.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Row from "../components/Row.astro";
---
<Base title="About">
  <div class="sm:flex sm:items-start sm:gap-8">
    <div class="flex-1">
      <p lang="ar" dir="rtl" class="font-arabic text-2xl text-muted dark:text-night-muted mb-2 text-left">أهلاً وسهلاً</p>
      <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl sm:text-5xl leading-[1.08] tracking-tight">Muna Abd El-Raziq</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 font-medium text-muted dark:text-night-muted">Ph.D. in Clinical Linguistics, CCC-SLP. Fulbright Postdoctoral Fellow, New York University.</p>
    </div>
    <img src="/muna.jpg" alt="Muna Abd El-Raziq" width="520" height="701" class="mt-6 sm:mt-0 w-32 sm:w-40 aspect-[3/4] object-cover rounded-2xl shadow-sm" />
  </div>

  <p class="mt-8 text-xl font-serif leading-snug max-w-[40ch]">I’m a linguist and speech-language pathologist. I study how autistic children who speak Arabic learn language, and why their language profiles differ so much from one child to the next.</p>
  <p class="mt-5 max-w-[65ch]">Arabic is diglossic: children speak a local dialect at home and meet Modern Standard Arabic mostly at school. That makes it a revealing language for asking how sounds, grammar, and vocabulary develop in autism, and how language connects to theory of mind and other thinking skills. My work combines experimental tasks, error analysis, eye-tracking, and network and cluster statistics, and it is shaped by more than fifteen years of clinical work with children and families.</p>

  <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-2" aria-label="Research areas">
    <span class="rounded-md px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[13px] font-semibold bg-[#f1e1c2] text-[#6a4b12] dark:bg-[#3d331f] dark:text-[#f0d79f]">Phonology</span>
    <span class="rounded-md px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[13px] font-semibold bg-[#e6dcf0] text-[#59397a] dark:bg-[#352b40] dark:text-[#dcc8f0]">Morphosyntax</span>
    <span class="rounded-md px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[13px] font-semibold bg-[#d7ebe3] text-[#22594a] dark:bg-[#1f3a32] dark:text-[#b5e3d2]">Vocabulary and diglossia</span>
    <span class="rounded-md px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[13px] font-semibold bg-[#f3dad6] text-[#7a3127] dark:bg-[#40272a] dark:text-[#f2c1b9]">Theory of mind and cognition</span>
    <span class="rounded-md px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[13px] font-semibold bg-[#dbe6f4] text-[#264d7a] dark:bg-[#223250] dark:text-[#bcd2f2]">Bilingualism</span>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <a href="/research/" class="rounded-lg bg-accent text-white dark:bg-night-accent dark:text-night px-5 py-2.5 font-semibold">Read my research</a>
    <a href="mailto:a.raziq.muna@gmail.com" class="rounded-lg border border-accent text-accent dark:border-night-accent dark:text-night-accent px-5 py-2.5 font-semibold">Email me</a>
  </div>

  <section class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-rule dark:border-night-rule bg-white/70 dark:bg-white/[0.03] p-6">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Right now</h2>
    <p>At NYU’s LEARN Lab with Prof. Sudha Arunachalam, I’m studying two-word combinations in autistic preschoolers and vocabulary in Arabic–English bilingual children using eye-tracking.</p>
    <p class="mt-3">Next: the Bloom Excellence Postdoctoral Fellowship at the University of Haifa (2026–2027).</p>
  </section>

  <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold mt-12 mb-4">My path</h2>
  <div class="space-y-5 max-w-[65ch]">
    <p>I completed my Ph.D. in Linguistics in Clinical Research at Bar-Ilan University, where my dissertation described the language profiles of Palestinian-Arabic-speaking children with autism, under the supervision of Prof. Natalia Meir and Prof. Elinor Saiegh-Haddad.</p>
    <p>Since then I have held postdoctoral fellowships at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem, the University of Fribourg in Switzerland, and New York University, where I am a Fulbright Postdoctoral Fellow. I am a member of the LACA network (Language Abilities in Children with Autism).</p>
    <p>Before and alongside research, I have worked as a speech-language pathologist since 2010, assessing and treating autistic children and children with developmental language disorder. That clinical work shapes the questions I ask.</p>
  </div>


  <Section title="In the media">
    <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/posts/october-issue-university-of-bath-psychology-ugcPost-7523457898962903040-Y4JP" class="block rounded-2xl border-t-4 border-[#8a63b3] dark:border-[#b597d6] bg-[#efe7f6] dark:bg-[#2a2333] p-6 no-underline hover:shadow-md transition-shadow">
      <span class="inline-block rounded-md px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[13px] font-semibold bg-[#e6dcf0] text-[#59397a] dark:bg-[#352b40] dark:text-[#dcc8f0]">Interview</span>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold mt-3 mb-1">Exploring Language and Autism: Why One Size Doesn’t Fit All</h3>
      <p class="text-[15px] text-muted dark:text-night-muted"><em>University of Bath Psychology Magazine</em>, Issue 08 (October issue), page 17. Interviewed by Mia Humphries.</p>
      <blockquote class="mt-4 font-serif text-lg border-l-4 border-[#8a63b3] dark:border-[#b597d6] pl-4">“There is no single profile.”</blockquote>
      <p class="mt-3">I talk about why autism cannot be understood through a single language profile, what research in Arabic adds to theories built mostly on English, and why each child’s strengths and needs should guide intervention.</p>
      <p class="mt-3 font-semibold text-accent dark:text-night-accent">Read the interview →</p>
    </a>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Positions">
    <Row when="2025–2026"><p>Fulbright Postdoctoral Fellow, Communicative Sciences and Disorders, New York University</p><p class="text-[15px] text-muted dark:text-night-muted">LEARN Lab, Prof. Sudha Arunachalam</p></Row>
    <Row when="2024–2025"><p>Postdoctoral Fellow, ABCCD Lab, University of Fribourg, Switzerland</p><p class="text-[15px] text-muted dark:text-night-muted">Prof. Stéphanie Durrleman</p></Row>
    <Row when="2024–2025"><p>Postdoctoral Fellow, Autism Child and Family Lab, Hebrew University of Jerusalem</p><p class="text-[15px] text-muted dark:text-night-muted">Dr. Judah Koller</p></Row>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Education">
    <Row when="2020–2024"><p>Ph.D. in Linguistics in Clinical Research, Bar-Ilan University</p><p class="text-[15px] text-muted dark:text-night-muted">Language Profiles of Palestinian-Arabic-Speaking Children with Autism Spectrum Disorder</p></Row>
    <Row when="2012–2015"><p>M.A. in Communication Disorders, Tel Aviv University</p><p class="text-[15px] text-muted dark:text-night-muted">Reading and bilingualism among Arabic–Hebrew bilinguals</p></Row>
    <Row when="2006–2010"><p>B.A. in Communication Disorders, Tel Aviv University</p></Row>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Selected awards">
    <Row when="2026–2027"><p>Bloom Excellence Postdoctoral Fellowship, University of Haifa</p></Row>
    <Row when="2026"><p>Simons Foundation Travel Award, Meeting on Language in Autism</p></Row>
    <Row when="2026"><p>NYU Postdoctoral Affairs Support Grant</p></Row>
    <Row when="2026"><p>ASHA Pathways Program (early-career grant mentoring)</p></Row>
    <Row when="2025"><p>Fulbright Postdoctoral Fellowship</p></Row>
    <Row when="2025"><p>Bar-Ilan University Postdoctoral Award for Women</p></Row>
    <Row when="2024"><p>President’s Prize for Three Published Papers, Bar-Ilan University</p></Row>
    <Row when="2023"><p>Dean’s Excellence Prize, Faculty of Humanities, Bar-Ilan University</p></Row>
    <Row when="2022"><p>Prof. Rahamimoff Travel Grant, U.S.–Israel Binational Science Foundation</p></Row>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Clinical experience">
    <Row when="2013–2025"><p>Speech-language pathologist in private practice, Tayibe</p></Row>
    <Row when="2022"><p>Communication and language development instructor, Clinical Center for Child Development, University of Haifa</p></Row>
    <Row when="2014–2019"><p>Speech-language pathologist, public kindergartens, Ministry of Education</p></Row>
    <Row when="2011–2014"><p>Speech-language pathologist, Child Development Center, Clalit Health Services, Jaffa</p></Row>
    <Row when="2010–2019"><p>Speech-language pathologist, autism kindergarten (ages 1–3), Association for Children at Risk</p></Row>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] text-muted dark:text-night-muted">Trained in ADOS-2 and ADI-R. Licensed speech-language pathologist (Israel, 2010).</p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Skills and languages">
    <p class="max-w-[65ch]">R (mixed-effects models, network and cluster analysis, eye-tracking data), CLAN transcription and coding, and EyeLink experiment design. I speak Arabic and Hebrew natively, and English.</p>
  </Section>
</Base>
